7. Detailed Feature Requirements
7.1 Registration, OTP & Sessions
·       Collect mobile number in E.164 format; collect profile fields after authentication.
·       OTP generated/verified server-side, short expiry, resend cooldown, attempt limits, rate limiting and abuse monitoring.
·       Never store OTP in plaintext. Use secure token storage backed by Android Keystore.
·       Support logout, session revocation, refresh-token rotation and optional device/session management.
·       Acceptance: expired, reused, incorrect and throttled OTP attempts fail safely.
7.2 Manual Bar Council Enrolment Verification
·       Capture Bar Council/state, enrolment number, enrolment date if applicable, name as shown on evidence and supporting documents.
·       Statuses: Not Submitted, Submitted, Under Review, More Information Required, Approved, Rejected, Suspended, Appeal Pending.
·       Admin review includes evidence viewer, checklist, reason codes, internal notes, reviewer and audit history.
·       Client defines evidence rules, reviewer training, service target, escalation and appeal process.
·       Use wording such as 'Platform verified – manual document review'; do not claim official Bar Council verification without authorization.
7.3 Advocate Profile & Digital ID
·       Profile: photo, display name, city/state, court/practice location, practice areas, years of practice (optional), bio and controlled contact visibility.
·       Identity/enrolment edits may trigger re-review.
·       Digital ID shows platform ID, photo, name, status and issue date; it is not government-issued ID.
·       QR resolves to a minimal public validation page; never encode sensitive personal data in QR.
7.4 SOS & Emergency Activation
·       Prominent SOS entry point; configurable hold threshold and countdown; easy cancel affordance.
·       Show category and disclosure of location/profile/notes/media shared and intended recipients.
·       Create unique incident ID; idempotency prevents duplicate incidents on repeated taps/retries.
·       Provide user-initiated call actions, incident group chat and one-to-one chat entry points.
·       Active incident banner remains until closure; closing requires confirmation/reason.
7.5 Emergency Communication
·       FCM push to registered devices and configured SMS provider/contact destinations where consent and policy permit.
·       Payload contains incident reference, category, sender display name and secure deep link; minimise lock-screen sensitive data.
·       Track Pending, Submitted/Accepted by provider, Failed, Unknown and Delivered/Read only when reliably supplied.
·       Use bounded retries, exponential backoff and idempotency; do not label provider acceptance as delivery.
7.6 Live Location & Google Maps
·       Request foreground location when needed with clear purpose rationale.
·       Background location only if essential, policy-compliant and approved; prefer visible foreground service during active SOS where appropriate.
·       Store coordinates with accuracy, timestamp and source; show last updated and accuracy radius.
·       Updates use configurable time/distance thresholds; stop when incident closes or consent is withdrawn under policy.
·       Handle approximate permission, denial, GPS disabled, stale fix, battery limits and network loss.
·       Google Maps shows incident/responder markers and can hand off navigation to Maps app.
7.7 Nearby Advocate Alerts
·       Only verified advocates who opted in and meet availability settings are eligible.
·       Sequential expansion: 2 km, then 5 km, then 10 km if configured timeout/minimum acceptance is not met.
·       Client must approve stage timeout, recipient cap and expansion criteria.
·       Deduplicate recipients across stages; apply cooldown and per-user rate limits.
·       Use server-side geospatial queries; do not expose directory-wide or recipient coordinates.
·       Decide whether exact incident location is withheld until acceptance; coarse location is the safer default.
7.8 Emergency Notes, Voice Notes & Media
·       Incident description and structured quick notes are user-entered.
·       Voice recording is explicit, visible, timed and user-controlled; no covert recording.
·       Photo/video uses system camera or Photo Picker where possible; show upload progress and retry.
·       Store media in encrypted object storage with type, size, duration, uploader, checksum and timestamps.
·       Apply file limits, malware scanning where feasible, signed URLs, access logging and retention policy.
·       Do not promise evidentiary admissibility; legal counsel must approve evidence handling.
7.9 Offline SOS
·       Detect offline state and clearly identify available fallbacks.
·       Where permitted, open SMS composer prefilled with recipient/message; user reviews and sends.
·       Do not assume silent SEND_SMS is allowed by Android/Google Play.
·       Offer phone-call action; disclose if GPS is unavailable or stale.
·       Queue minimum encrypted metadata only if policy allows; label local save/SMS handoff distinctly from server-confirmed SOS.
7.10 Categories A & B
·       A: urgent response target under 30 minutes; B: response may be required after one hour.
·       Targets are internal operational goals, not guarantees.
·       Store category, selection/change actor and timestamps.
·       Allow escalation during active incident; downgrade may require confirmation or admin policy.
7.11 Responder Status
·       States: Alerted, Accepted, On the Way, Reached, Declined, Cancelled, Timed Out.
·       Allowed transitions: Alerted→Accepted/Declined; Accepted→On the Way/Cancelled; On the Way→Reached/Cancelled.
·       Record actor, server timestamp, optional note and permitted location.
·       Notify incident owner; show responder name, status and last update.
·       Responder live location is not shared broadly by default.
7.12 Police & Senior Officer Directory
·       Search by state, district, city, station, officer, rank or designated role.
·       Fields include agency, address, public phone/email if approved, service area, source and verified date.
·       Client supplies/validates data; admin supports import, edit, deactivate and audit.
·       Display last-verified date and data freshness disclaimer; do not imply police-system integration.
7.13 SOS History & Incident Log
·       List incidents by date, category, status and area; provide filters and pagination.
·       Detail includes timeline, alert channel status, responder updates, notes and authorized media.
·       Access limited to owner, permitted participants and authorized operations/admin roles.
·       Retention, export, deletion and legal hold rules require client/legal approval.
·       Log sensitive record and media access.
7.14 Privacy & Consent
·       Separate consent for nearby alerts, SOS location, profile discoverability, feed visibility and optional communications.
·       Explain permissions contextually; denial must not block unrelated features.
·       Show active sharing state and withdrawal controls with consequences.
·       Provide policy links and data access/correction/deletion request route as applicable.
·       Record consent version, timestamp, purpose, screen and withdrawal history.
7.15 Professional Networking
·       Feed supports text posts, permitted attachments, comments, likes, sharing and connections.
·       Optional court/bar/location tags; never expose precise home/current location by default.
·       Visibility controls, preview, report, block, mute and moderation tools.
·       Pagination, abuse controls and duplicate-action prevention.
·       Warn users not to post confidential client data, privileged communications or sensitive case documents.
7.16 Premium WhatsApp Sharing & Broadcast
·       Direct sharing via Android Sharesheet/WhatsApp intent where installed.
·       Premium entitlement checked server-side and restored across devices.
·       No unsupported personal-account bulk messaging automation or bypassing WhatsApp limits.
·       WhatsApp Business Platform requires approved business account, templates, opt-in and provider integration.
·       Handoff does not prove delivery; show truthful completion state.
7.17 Legal Blog & Judgments
·       Content includes legal updates, blogs, Supreme Court/High Court judgments and editorial explainers.
·       Metadata: title, court, case/citation, decision/publication date, source URL/document, author, tags and review status.
·       Workflow: Draft→Editorial Review→Legal Review→Approved→Published; correction/unpublish actions audited.
·       Attribute official source and link to original; review copyright/terms before reproducing material.
·       Distinguish editorial summary from original judgment; informational-not-legal-advice disclaimer.
·       Daily cadence depends on source availability and editorial staffing; do not promise exhaustive coverage without SLA.
